 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>As well as brown bobs so boss they featured on every model, Ashley Williams delivered a collection of whimsically twisted domesticity at the ICA this afternoon. The vibe was <em>Grey Gardens</em> somehow transported from East Hampton to the ’80s and ’90s Midwest. Fabrics were crumpled like wallpaper pulled down by gravity, hordes of soft toys were stuffed into hosiery, and what looked like long-gone kids’ bedsheets featuring characters adjacent to Strawberry Shortcake and Miffy were cut into house dresses, camisoles and vests.</p><p>Especially relishable garments included a Quant-ish minidress in Scooby-Doo florals and a chalk stripe wool dress, both cut to accommodate what Caitlin Moran once described as a Simon and Garfunkel bosom: one running high, the other going low. Frill-fringed hot-pink Barbara Cartland silks were heaped forward into unmade-bed-like backless dresses. Track tops were merged with peachy viscose skirts, or belted and hemmed high in cheerleader pleats. Somehow-sad 1980s Sue Ellen Ewing dresses were sleeved from bold shoulder to gloved fingertip.</p><p>Williams is a hybridist who applies an archly surreal filter to saccharine signifiers of girlhood and femininity, making them weird enough to wear without feeling infantilized or subordinated by. This clever cake-and-eat-it approach has won her a solid IYKYK fanbase of women who relate: she’s currently doing particularly strong business in the basement at Dover Street Market London.</p></div>",
